KNOWLEDGE=[
{"id":"ipcc-ar6-syr","title":"IPCC AR6 Synthesis Report: climate science, impacts and response","topic":"climate mitigation adaptation greenhouse gas","text":"The IPCC Sixth Assessment synthesis integrates evidence on observed warming, human influence, climate impacts, adaptation limits and mitigation pathways. It finds that human activities have unequivocally caused global warming and that limiting warming requires rapid, deep and sustained greenhouse gas emission reductions. Adaptation and mitigation are complementary responses; risks and feasible responses vary by region and vulnerability.","source":"IPCC (2023), Climate Change 2023: Synthesis Report","url":"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/syr/"},
{"id":"unep-emissions-gap-2024","title":"UNEP Emissions Gap Report 2024","topic":"emissions gap climate policy pledges pathways","text":"The UNEP Emissions Gap assessment compares projected global greenhouse gas emissions under current policies and national pledges with emissions pathways consistent with temperature goals. It discusses ambition and implementation gaps and evaluates policy options across sectors. Consult report tables for scenario assumptions and numerical estimates; values should not be inferred from this summary.","source":"UNEP (2024), Emissions Gap Report 2024","url":"https://www.unep.org/resources/emissions-gap-report-2024"},
{"id":"iea-world-energy-outlook","title":"IEA World Energy Outlook 2024","topic":"energy electricity renewables fossil fuels grid","text":"The International Energy Agency's World Energy Outlook analyses energy security, investment, technology deployment and policy scenarios. It examines electricity demand, clean energy deployment, fuels and implications of different stated-policy and announced-pledge pathways. Scenario outputs are conditional projections, not predictions; compare assumptions and definitions before using figures.","source":"International Energy Agency (2024), World Energy Outlook 2024","url":"https://www.iea.org/reports/world-energy-outlook-2024"},
{"id":"ipbes-global-assessment","title":"IPBES Global Assessment on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","topic":"biodiversity ecosystems habitat species nature","text":"The IPBES Global Assessment synthesizes evidence on biodiversity and nature's contributions to people. It identifies direct drivers of biodiversity loss including land- and sea-use change, direct exploitation, climate change, pollution and invasive alien species. Responses include transformative changes in institutions, production and consumption, conservation, restoration and sustainable use.","source":"IPBES (2019), Global Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","url":"https://www.ipbes.net/global-assessment"},
{"id":"unep-global-resources-outlook","title":"UNEP Global Resources Outlook 2024","topic":"materials resource use circular economy waste","text":"The Global Resources Outlook assesses global extraction and processing of natural resources and their environmental and climate impacts. It explores pathways for reducing resource pressures through demand-side measures, material efficiency, circularity and changes in energy, food and built-environment systems. Interpret indicators using the report's system boundaries and definitions.","source":"UNEP International Resource Panel (2024), Global Resources Outlook 2024","url":"https://www.unep.org/resources/Global-Resource-Outlook-2024"},
{"id":"fao-sofia","title":"FAO State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2024","topic":"fisheries aquaculture food systems aquatic ecosystems","text":"FAO's SOFIA report provides a global overview of fisheries and aquaculture, production, employment, food security and sustainability challenges. It distinguishes capture fisheries from aquaculture and reports trends using defined statistical categories. Country and species-level comparisons should use the report's underlying tables and metadata.","source":"FAO (2024), The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/publications/sofia/2024/en"},
{"id":"world-bank-climate","title":"World Bank Climate Change Knowledge Portal","topic":"climate data country profiles climate risk","text":"The World Bank Climate Change Knowledge Portal provides country and subnational climate information, historical observations, projections and climate-risk context. Indicators are model- and scenario-dependent. Inspect geography, time period, model ensemble, emissions scenario and variable definitions before comparing values.","source":"World Bank, Climate Change Knowledge Portal","url":"https://climateknowledgeportal.worldbank.org/"},
{"id":"our-world-in-data-co2","title":"Our World in Data: CO2 and Greenhouse Gas Emissions","topic":"emissions carbon dioxide greenhouse gases country data","text":"Our World in Data compiles and visualizes CO2 and greenhouse gas emissions indicators from named primary sources. Definitions differ across territorial, consumption-based and land-use categories. Inspect each chart's source, indicator definition, unit, coverage and last updated date before interpreting country comparisons.","source":"Our World in Data, CO2 and Greenhouse Gas Emissions","url":"https://ourworldindata.org/co2-and-greenhouse-gas-emissions"},
{"id":"climate-01","title":"Climate change fundamentals","topic":"climate","text":"Climate change refers to long-term shifts in temperatures and weather patterns. Human activities, especially burning fossil fuels, increase heat-trapping greenhouse gas concentrations. Mitigation reduces emissions or increases removals; adaptation reduces harm from climate impacts.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; see IPCC synthesis","url":"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/syr/"},
{"id":"energy-01","title":"Renewable electricity","topic":"energy","text":"Solar, wind and hydropower generate electricity from replenished energy flows. Lifecycle impacts depend on materials, siting, grid integration, storage and end-of-life management. Comparing technologies requires consistent lifecycle boundaries and functional units.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; see IEA World Energy Outlook","url":"https://www.iea.org/reports/world-energy-outlook-2024"},
{"id":"biodiversity-01","title":"Biodiversity and ecosystems","topic":"biodiversity","text":"Biodiversity includes variation within species, between species and across ecosystems. Habitat conversion, overexploitation, invasive species, pollution and climate change are major pressures. Conservation combines protected areas, restoration and sustainable management.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; see IPBES Global Assessment","url":"https://www.ipbes.net/global-assessment"},
{"id":"waste-01","title":"Waste prevention and circularity","topic":"waste","text":"The waste hierarchy prioritizes prevention and reuse before recycling, recovery and disposal. Circular economy strategies design out waste, keep products and materials in use, and regenerate natural systems. Recycling depends on collection, sorting, material quality and local infrastructure.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; see UNEP Global Resources Outlook","url":"https://www.unep.org/resources/Global-Resource-Outlook-2024"},
{"id":"water-01","title":"Water stewardship","topic":"water","text":"Water stress depends on withdrawals relative to available renewable supplies and local ecological needs. Efficient irrigation, leakage reduction, watershed restoration and reuse can help, but interventions should be assessed at basin scale.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; see UN-Water resources","url":"https://www.unwater.org/"}]
